# Routing rules for the static deploy (Netlify reads this file from the
# published directory; `public/` is copied verbatim into `.output/public`).
#
# Context — the two rules below must stay together:
#
# 1. Trailing slash. `nitro.prerender.autoSubfolderIndex` is off (nuxt.config.ts),
#    so a route is written as `services.html` and Netlify serves `/services` with
#    a 200 and no redirect — which is the URL the canonical, hreflang and sitemap
#    output all advertise. A `services/index.html` directory index would instead
#    be answered with a 301 to `/services/`, adding a redirect to every internal
#    link and pointing the 200 URL away from its own canonical. The slash form is
#    therefore redirected back to the canonical path.
#
# 2. The catch-all is REQUIRED, not decorative. A request that matches no file
#    and no rule is answered by Netlify with a 301 to the *same* URL, so an
#    unknown path spins until the visitor's browser gives up with
#    "Maximum redirects followed" (reproduced on a Netlify deploy, including a
#    deploy carrying no rules at all). Resolving the miss explicitly to
#    /404.html with a 404 status makes every request end in a real status code.
#    This has to live here rather than in netlify.toml: the same rule declared as
#    `[[redirects]] status = 404` there was silently ignored, and the loop stayed.
#
# Both rules are non-forced, so a request that does match a file is still served
# the file: /services, /sitemap.xml, /robots.txt, the hashed `_nuxt` assets and
# the `_ipx` image variants are never affected.
/*/  /:splat  301
/*   /404.html  404
